	<tr><td style="padding: 1px 1px 0 1px;">
		<table width="100%" cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" border="0">
			<tr><td class="windowbg">
				<table width="100%" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="table-layout: fixed;">
					<tr>
						<td valign="top" width="16%" rowspan="2" style="overflow: hidden;">
							<b><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?action=profile;u=1887" title="View the profile of J">J</a></b>
							<div class="smalltext">
								Newbie<br />
								<img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><br />
								Posts: 4<br />
								<br />
								
								
								
								<br />
								<a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?action=profile;u=1887"><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/icons/profile_sm.gif" alt="View Profile" title="View Profile" border="0" /></a>
							</div>
						</td>
						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2452.msg29491#msg29491"><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2452.msg29491#msg29491">Re: Blue Scholars goin Big</a></b>
									<div class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #30 on:</b> June 05, 2007, 10:21:09 am &#187;</div></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;">This is a fascinating thread.&nbsp; For some time I’ve been reading what y’all have to say about the local hip hop scene in Seattle and music in general.&nbsp; I would like to speak to several things that have been mentioned in this thread.&nbsp; Now, I am totally aware of the fact that we are personally interpreting a few words of others on these threads which may lead to misinterpretation.&nbsp; So I apologize in advance if I am doing the latter.<br /><br />It seems that many of you are interested in the Blue Scholars broadening their fan base if that includes listeners who are hip to the messages in the music.&nbsp; However, there are a bunch of folks out there that listen to hip hop for the beats and don’t pay much attention to the lyrics (and of course, those who listen to fit in with a crowd, piss off their parents, etc.).&nbsp; If your music puts a smile on someone’s face, gets them to rock back and forth, or makes them forget about a troubling day, more power to you.&nbsp; Yet, like I mentioned before, attracting fans that process the lyrical messages seems to be of value to many of you (i.e. uphold the integrity of the music, movement).&nbsp; <br /><br />I want to pose a few questions to everyone based on what Sabzi mentioned earlier.&nbsp; &nbsp;If your goal is to continue to build a “conscious” fan base, how do you go about doing that (as a fan turning on others or as an artist)?&nbsp; More specifically, what differentiates the fans that are enjoying the beats and those that are paying attention to the messages in the music?&nbsp; What does it take to turn on a listener who may have counter views to alternative messages in the music?&nbsp; Finally, what does it take to move someone from processing these messages to taking action (e.g. getting involved in community building, contributing to a larger discourse, etc.) I want to mention here too that I am asking these questions straight up, not stating that these are the intentions of the Blue Scholars or any of you on this board.&nbsp; If our goal is to be involved in a movement of change and positive progression, I think this is can be an invaluable dialogue.<br /></div>
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td valign="bottom" class="smalltext" width="85%">
							<table width="100%" border="0" style="table-layout: fixed;"><tr>
