							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2452.msg29491#msg29491">Quote from: J on June 05, 2007, 10:21:09 am</a></div><div class="quote">This is a fascinating thread.  For some time I’ve been reading what y’all have to say about the local hip hop scene in Seattle and music in general.  I would like to speak to several things that have been mentioned in this thread.  Now, I am totally aware of the fact that we are personally interpreting a few words of others on these threads which may lead to misinterpretation.  So I apologize in advance if I am doing the latter.<br /><br />It seems that many of you are interested in the Blue Scholars broadening their fan base if that includes listeners who are hip to the messages in the music.  However, there are a bunch of folks out there that listen to hip hop for the beats and don’t pay much attention to the lyrics (and of course, those who listen to fit in with a crowd, piss off their parents, etc.).  If your music puts a smile on someone’s face, gets them to rock back and forth, or makes them forget about a troubling day, more power to you.  Yet, like I mentioned before, attracting fans that process the lyrical messages seems to be of value to many of you (i.e. uphold the integrity of the music, movement).  <br /><br />I want to pose a few questions to everyone based on what Sabzi mentioned earlier.   If your goal is to continue to build a “conscious” fan base, how do you go about doing that (as a fan turning on others or as an artist)?  More specifically, what differentiates the fans that are enjoying the beats and those that are paying attention to the messages in the music?  What does it take to turn on a listener who may have counter views to alternative messages in the music?  Finally, what does it take to move someone from processing these messages to taking action (e.g. getting involved in community building, contributing to a larger discourse, etc.) I want to mention here too that I am asking these questions straight up, not stating that these are the intentions of the Blue Scholars or any of you on this board.  If our goal is to be involved in a movement of change and positive progression, I think this is can be an invaluable dialogue.<br /><br /></div><br />very good questions J.&nbsp; you are right to point out the diversity of hip hop listeners and the different reasons why we on this thread see the value of Blue Scholars &quot;goin Big&quot;.&nbsp; as far as people that may have counter views to alternative messages in the music, people start somewhere.&nbsp; music has shown that it is a powerful thing in tapping into different feelings, emotions, and interpretations for people.&nbsp; it seems to me that many that even agree with the messages have a tough time applying themselves to address the issues.&nbsp; so, with the messages i am hearing in blue scholars shows the spectrum from being an &quot;ordinary guy&quot; to applying one&#039;s self in different ways (theory into practice).&nbsp; <br /><br />along the lines of listeners that listen for the beats (as many do), that is part of the blue scholars appeal.&nbsp; dope music (lyrics and beats, etc.) from blue scholars makes it applicable to such a broad base.&nbsp; we can&#039;t assume that people are going to take the lyrics and the message and run with it.&nbsp; many may even deny it.&nbsp; it seems we all have some music if not more that we like to listen to but don&#039;t agree with the message.&nbsp; but, it doesn&#039;t stop us from still listening.&nbsp; &nbsp;like chris rock said about the song &quot;get low&quot;...many females when asked about the lyrics and being labelled as different things, he got the response of &quot;he ain&#039;t talking bout me&quot;.&nbsp; so, with all this said and done, we see a reinforcement of many problems in our communities through these songs.&nbsp; the question now becomes, will we see a positive shift in changing these problems in our community if artists like blue scholars &quot;get Big&quot;?<br /><br /></div>
