							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2452.msg29491#msg29491">Quote from: J on June 05, 2007, 10:21:09 am</a></div><div class="quote">  <br />I want to pose a few questions to everyone based on what Sabzi mentioned earlier.   If your goal is to continue to build a “conscious” fan base, how do you go about doing that (as a fan turning on others or as an artist)?  More specifically, what differentiates the fans that are enjoying the beats and those that are paying attention to the messages in the music?  What does it take to turn on a listener who may have counter views to alternative messages in the music?  Finally, what does it take to move someone from processing these messages to taking action (e.g. getting involved in community building, contributing to a larger discourse, etc.) I want to mention here too that I am asking these questions straight up, not stating that these are the intentions of the Blue Scholars or any of you on this board.  If our goal is to be involved in a movement of change and positive progression, I think this is can be an invaluable dialogue.<br /><br /></div><br />good posts coming in and great points all around....keep on keepin on as Lyte would say.  First off, I guess there is no clear cut way to get folks to embrace the messages applied by Massline and integrate them into an everyday lifestyle.  The music will be percieved the way the listener wants to take it, but examples can be expressed through other means.  Whether its giving more examples of community work being done by the group or just showing they are involved, which there hasnt been any shortage of thus far.  I guess using myself as an example provides a template of what can be hoped for from future listeners.  Started with someone putting me on to them on AHH, loved them first listen, but over time began to really feel the mantras that were being pushed.  That in turn sparked my interest in Gabe, RA, and Ab Creole...leading further to Macklemore, Dyme Def etc.  Months later I am becoming more involved in the boards, becoming hip to not only musical conversations, but also becoming aware of social, political, and other issues.  It is gratifying to feel like you are actively involved with people who fully embrace this kind of music.  In turn, indirectly massline has caused a shift in my thinking and my willingness to share ideas and gripes with people in my life as well as strangers, instead of staying silent and coping.<br /><br />As far as progressing the recognition of Massline, I think a good start would be some sort of fan/board member driven awareness group.  I understand there is a street team area for people to express interest in flyering and whatnot, but Im speaking more on a national level.  As the board member numbers increase, so does the amount of varying places in which we reside.  If you are looking to just get the name out there (in hopes of adding a bit more to the movement, which may lead to those ideal type fans), take advantage of whats here.  Like saba said, with only a few people, its gotta be hard for them to meet the musical, business, and social demands of Massline.  Maybe something as small as providing a general artist poster or flyer about upcoming albums or something would be a start...then those outside of seattle could poster where they are at, sparking interest in different regions.  Id love to just burn tons of cd&#039;s and pass them out to people, but we should spread the music the right way.  Maybe people could send the Scholars listings of local record shops so they could send these places cd&#039;s.  Sparking interest in those who work at local shops would be a big piece in spreading by word of mouth.  Local spots usually have &quot;Recommended&quot; or &quot;Whats hot&quot; boards, usually determined by those who work in the store according to taste.  These are all small ideas, but they just might help the progression nationally for these guys, even though the album itself should take care of that.  But when I visited seattle I heard of a little something called &quot;east coast bias&quot; in major media, sports, etc....hopefully you guys wont get screwed out of a championship on SuperBowl tuesday....<br /><br />any other ideas fellow forum members?</div>
